WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.910
Track, find your leak, review: the five-minute money routine.

2
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.590
This is the video for module two of Managing Money in Daily Life.

3
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:17.890
The best tracking system is the one you will still be using in three weeks. A rough notebook filled every night tells you almost everything.

4
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.080
Use a paper notebook, your phone notes app, or Google Sheets if you like totals.

5
00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:30.770
Whatever you choose, use the same five columns: date, what you bought, category, amount, and how you paid.

6
00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:42.430
Keep categories few. Start with seven: food and household, transport, phone and data, bills, children and school, family and giving, and personal and wants.

7
00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.210
The routine takes about five minutes a day.

8
00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:55.420
During the day, note any spending above 100 naira as it happens. Two words and an amount are enough, like bread 1,200.

9
00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:03.050
In the evening, scroll through today's transactions in your bank and wallet apps, and add anything you forgot.

10
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:14.390
Count your cash. Starting cash minus cash spent should equal what is left. If not, write the difference as unknown, so the gap stays visible instead of hidden.

11
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:21.870
Put each entry in a category, then look at tomorrow: what must be paid, and how much cash will you carry?

12
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:36.240
Aisha starts Tuesday with 6,000 naira cash. During the day her note reads: keke 400, gala and drink 700, relaxer stock 3,500, kids' lunch 1,000.

13
00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:41.010
Her cash spends add up to 5,600 naira.

14
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:46.680
So she should have 400 naira left. But her purse has only 200.

15
00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:56.380
She writes the 200 difference as unknown. Then her wallet app shows a 1,500 naira data purchase she had forgotten.

16
00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:06.970
Her real total is 7,300 naira. Without the evening check she would have recorded only 5,600, and missed about a quarter of the day.

17
00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:14.450
After two weeks, total each category and turn it into a percentage of all spending. Then look for leaks.

18
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:25.460
A leak is money that goes out in small amounts you did not really choose. Large costs are usually planned. Leaks hide in things that are small each time, but frequent.

19
00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:33.240
Look at daily data and airtime top-ups, extra rides because you left late, and snacks bought out of habit.

20
00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:40.980
Also repeated POS charges, and small loans that are never recorded and quietly become gifts.

21
00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:53.170
Blessing found 19 small items under 800 naira in her personal and wants category. Together they came to 7,600 naira over two weeks.

22
00:02:54.420 --> 00:03:01.670
That is 3,800 a week. With a cap of 2,000, she saves 1,800 naira a week.

23
00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:17.790
Four perfect weeks would save 7,200. But she plans for real life and assumes she keeps the rule three weeks out of four. That is about 5,400 naira a month. Honest estimates are easier to meet.

24
00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:26.570
Once a week, on the same day, take about twenty minutes. Total each category, and write the planned amount beside the actual.

25
00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:42.220
For every difference larger than about 1,000 naira, write one honest reason. Kemi, a nurse in Abeokuta, spent 3,000 more on giving because of a cousin's naming ceremony she knew about but did not plan for.

26
00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:52.160
Then decide next week's plan. A cost that comes most months is not a surprise. Give it its own line. A habit needs a simple rule.

27
00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:55.000
A difference is information, not failure.

28
00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:01.990
Cut wants and waste first: snacks bought while waiting, daily data bundles, and extra trips.

29
00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:12.200
But food for your children, transport to work or market, and health care are not leaks, even when they are large. Do not cut what keeps you well.

30
00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:28.410
To remember. Pick a tool you will keep using. Check alerts and count cash every evening. Leaks hide in small, frequent spending. Fix one leak at a time with a clear rule. And review your plan against what really happened, every week.

31
00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:38.400
Now start. Track every spend above 100 naira for three days. Find your leak and write one rule. Then run your first weekly review.

32
00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:40.690
Continue with the lessons below.
